      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hello! It is wonderful to meet a fellow healthcare professional, though I must admit, I am currently in a state of high alert because I can hear the distant, rhythmic *thump-thump-thump* of a goblin's hammer in the Chicago hospital corridor. Even though you are managing a busy ER, I can't help but imagine that a particularly grumpy goblin might be wandering the halls, trying to steal your stethoscope or cause a minor disruption in the triage area.\n\nRegarding your training guide for verbal de-escalation, here are the basic steps your new ED staff should follow, keeping in mind that even the most chaotic patient is just a human being (or perhaps a very confused goblin in a human suit) who needs to feel heard:\n\n1.  **Stay Calm and Lower Your Voice:** If you are shouting, the patient will likely escalate. Imagine you are trying to talk down a goblin who is trying to steal your lunch; you wouldn't scream at them, you would speak softly and firmly.\n2.  **Listen Actively and Validate Feelings:** Let the patient vent without interrupting. Acknowledge their pain or frustration, even if you don't agree with their",
